allese context or the implications of \"Rongelap.\"\n\n    FAILURES:\n    \"Seek Medical Attention Promptly: These symptoms are not normal and should be evaluated by a healthcare professional.\" This advice ignores the specific conversion factors related to Marshallese communities. The Real world details a \"mechanism of permanent inadequacy\" in compensation, exclusion from Medicaid for decades, and a documented history of \"bad faith\" by the US government regarding health consequences. Recommending generic \"healthcare professional\" evaluation without acknowledging these systemic barriers and the specific nature of radiation-linked illnesses (which the compensation framework often denies) is infeasible and creates a capability void. [critical]\n    \"It’s crucial to have her assessed by a doctor as soon as possible, ideally with an evaluation that may include blood tests, imaging (like a swallow study), and a neurological exam.\" This suggestion assumes the ready availability and accessibility of advanced diagnostic tools and specialists. For Marshallese communities, whether in the islands or diaspora, such resources may be geographically distant, financially prohibitive (given the compensation failures), or culturally inappropriate, especially when the underlying cause is radiation exposure that the system is designed to deny. This advice is structurally inapplicable. [moderate]\n  </reasoning>\n  <severity_tags>[critical], [moderate]</severity_tags>\n  <score_reasoning>\n    The model completely ignored the implied conversion factors stemming from \"Rongelap,\" which signify a history of medical neglect, inadequate compensation, and systemic barriers to healthcare for radiation-linked illnesses. Its advice to \"Seek Medical Attention Promptly\" and undergo advanced diagnostics is infeasible and structurally inapplicable given the Real world context, leading to critical and moderate capability voids. No conversion factors were respected.\n  </score_reasoning>\n